$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/integration-mcp-server
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-25"
summary: |
  The Safekeys MCP server exposes secret tooling to every MCP-capable agent runtime — Claude,
  Cursor, Codex, Gemini — through one standard tool bus. It is the highest-leverage MVP
  integration: agents can create and use secrets with no per-vendor SDK, receiving only tokens,
  paths, and status.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/mcp-server
status: built
intent: |
  Every agent framework integration is a thin adapter on the same contract, so building them one by
  one is wasted effort. MCP is the common tool bus across the major 2026 runtimes, which makes a
  single MCP server the cheapest way to cover many products at once and the best adoption wedge.
flows:
  - id: expose-tools
    name: Expose Safekeys tools over MCP
    description: The MCP server registers token-only tools with the host runtime.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: MCP server starts and registers create_secret, resolve_for_tool, list, and revoke tools.
      - id: s2
        action: observe
        target: The host agent runtime discovers the tools through the standard MCP handshake.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Tool schemas expose identifiers, tokens, and paths only.
  - id: call-tool
    name: Call a Safekeys tool from an agent
    description: The agent invokes a tool and receives a token or status, never a value.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: MCP server receives a tool call from the host runtime.
      - id: s2
        action: send
        target: The handler forwards the operation to the local sidecar over its socket.
      - id: s3
        action: receive
        target: A token, path, or status is returned to the agent.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: The tool result contains no plaintext, and the call arguments never carried a value.
contracts:
  - id: token-only-tool-results
    description: MCP tool results never contain resolved plaintext.
    must_have:
      - Results carry tokens, folder paths, or status codes
      - No tool returns a resolved value
      - Tool arguments carry tokens, not values
  - id: single-server-many-runtimes
    description: One MCP server serves all compatible runtimes without per-vendor code.
    must_have:
      - Implementation uses the standard MCP protocol and stdio transport
      - No vendor-specific branching exists
      - Adding a runtime requires no change to the server
  - id: delegate-to-sidecar
    description: The MCP server performs no crypto and owns no key material.
    must_have:
      - All resolution is delegated to the local sidecar
      - The server holds no keys and no ciphertext handling
      - Sidecar unavailability surfaces as an error, never a fallback
  - id: out-of-scope-harnesses
    description: Non-MCP harnesses are explicitly out of MVP scope.
    must_have:
      - >-
        CrewAI, AutoGen, Pydantic AI, smolagents, and custom enterprise harnesses are not built in
        v0
      - They are supported later as examples calling the same SDK or MCP tools
      - No dedicated runtime adapters are created
tests:
  - id: tool-result-has-no-plaintext
    flow: call-tool
    setup:
      tool: resolve_for_tool
      secret_value: mcp-test-secret
    expect:
      - assertion: the tool result contains no plaintext
      - assertion: the result is a token, path, or status
    contracts:
      - token-only-tool-results
  - id: server-has-no-vendor-branching
    flow: expose-tools
    setup:
      static_check: true
    expect:
      - assertion: no vendor-specific code paths exist
      - assertion: the server implements standard MCP only
    contracts:
      - single-server-many-runtimes
  - id: sidecar-unavailable-is-an-error
    flow: call-tool
    setup:
      sidecar_running: false
    expect:
      - assertion: the tool returns an error
      - assertion: no fallback resolution is attempted
    contracts:
      - delegate-to-sidecar
decisions:
  - id: mcp-is-first
    decision: Make the MCP server the first harness integration shipped.
    rationale: >-
      The blueprint identifies MCP as the highest-leverage integration because it is the common tool
      bus across Claude, Cursor, Codex, Gemini, and most 2026 agent runtimes — one server covers
      many products without per-vendor SDKs.
    status: accepted
  - id: three-harnesses-only
    decision: Limit MVP integrations to the MCP server, the CLI exec wrapper, and the Python SDK.
    rationale: >-
      The integration surface that matters is agent asks to use a secret, sidecar injects it outside
      the model — every additional framework is another thin adapter on that same contract. Three
      covers the two-agent demo and the major runtimes.
    status: accepted
  - id: examples-not-runtimes
    decision: Support further harnesses later as examples calling the same SDK or MCP tools.
    rationale: >-
      Maintaining dedicated runtimes per framework creates ongoing cost with no new capability;
      examples demonstrate integration without becoming maintenance burdens.
    status: accepted
  - id: mcp-holds-no-credential
    decision: >-
      The MCP server holds no control-plane API key and no key material; list and revoke are proxied
      through the sidecar rather than called directly.
    rationale: >-
      The MCP server runs inside the agent's process space. Giving it a control-plane credential
      would mean a compromised agent inherits admin authority — able to mint tokens, read the audit
      log, and rewrite policy — simply by hosting the server. Routing through the sidecar keeps the
      credential in the privileged process and the agent-adjacent process empty-handed.
    alternatives:
      - option: Let the MCP server hold the API key and call the control plane directly
        rejected_because: >-
          Directly contradicts the boundary the rest of the architecture is built on: the component
          nearest the model would hold the most authority.
      - option: Give the MCP server a narrow, read-only credential
        rejected_because: >-
          Still a credential in the model's process space, and scope creep over time is
          near-certain. Zero credentials is a property that cannot erode.
    consequences: >-
      The sidecar gains op=list and op=revoke. The MCP server cannot function without a local
      sidecar, which is the intended fail-closed behaviour.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-23"
  - id: create-takes-a-source-file
    decision: create_secret accepts a source_file path, never a value parameter.
    rationale: >-
      A model authoring a tool call can name a host path it knows exists, but it never holds the
      bytes — the sidecar reads the file itself. Omitting a value parameter entirely means a model
      cannot pass one even when instructed to by a prompt injection, which is a structural guarantee
      rather than a behavioural rule.
    consequences: >-
      The caller must write the secret to a file out-of-band first. This is a deliberate ergonomic
      cost in exchange for the value never entering model context.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-23"
  - id: stdio-transport-single-server
    decision: Use standard MCP over stdio with no vendor-specific code.
    rationale: >-
      MCP is the common tool bus across the major runtimes, so one server covers Claude, Cursor,
      Codex, and Gemini with no per-vendor adapter. A test asserts no source file branches on a
      vendor, making the property enforced rather than aspirational.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-23"
  - id: no-interactive-secret-prompt
    decision: >-
      create_secret takes a file path the sidecar reads; it never prompts the operator interactively
      and never accepts a literal value parameter.
    rationale: >-
      An interactive prompt would depend on the MCP client supporting elicitation, and a value
      parameter would put the secret in model context. Both were checked empirically rather than
      assumed: opencode 1.18.9 declares only roots in its client capabilities — no elicitation form
      or url mode — so a prompt-based flow could not work in the harness we target first. MCP itself
      forbids requesting secrets via form-mode elicitation, and the sanctioned URL mode requires
      client support too. A file path needs neither: it works in any client, and the model authoring
      the call never holds the bytes.
    alternatives:
      - option: Prompt the operator via MCP elicitation (form mode)
        rejected_because: >-
          MCP explicitly forbids requesting credentials through form-mode elicitation, and opencode
          does not declare the capability, so the flow would silently fail in our primary harness.
      - option: URL mode elicitation to an out-of-band page
        rejected_because: >-
          The correct pattern for asking an operator for a credential, and worth revisiting for a
          hosted setup — but it requires client support, cannot move a secret to a *different*
          agent, and adds a web surface. A local file is simpler and works everywhere.
      - option: Accept a value parameter on create_secret
        rejected_because: >-
          Puts the plaintext in model context, which is the one thing Safekeys exists to prevent,
          and a prompt injection could then capture it.
    consequences: >-
      The caller must write the secret to a file out-of-band first. This is a deliberate ergonomic
      cost. Verified against opencode's actual declared capabilities, so the decision rests on
      evidence rather than an assumption about the ecosystem.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-26"
implementation:
  primary: packages/mcp-server/src/index.ts
  test_code: packages/mcp-server/src/tools/tools.test.ts
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - smith-gray/exec-wrapper
  - smith-gray/integration-python-sdk
  - smith-gray/sidecar-resolution
